## What is wrong

When the child exits during the handshake, `_on_close` runs first: it records the exit (`lib/MCP/Hub/Upstream/Stdio.pm:269-272`), rejects the pending handshake request and deletes `{stopping}` (`:275`). The rejection then reaches the `catch` in `start_p` (`Stdio.pm:70-79`), which records the same exit a second time (`:74`) and sets `{stopping} = 1` again (`:75`) for a child that is already gone. That flag stays set, so the next unexpected exit is treated as a deliberate stop: no warning is logged and `_on_close` skips its own `_record_exit`.

## Reproduce

    command => '/bin/false'
    start #1 -> state=stopped exits=2 stopping=1
    start #2 -> state=failed  exits=3 stopping=1

The rule documented and tested (`t/stdio.t:117`, "failed after three quick exits") is three exits within the window; a server that fails at startup is locked out after two attempts and needs `mcp-hub refresh NAME`. The stale `stopping` flag also hides the "child exited" warning of the following crash.

## Done

- A startup exit is recorded exactly once (the `catch` records only when `_on_close` has not already handled this pid, e.g. on a handshake timeout where the child is still alive).
- `{stopping}` is only set while a pid exists.
- `t/stdio.t` gains a case with a command that exits immediately: state is `stopped` after one and two attempts, `failed` after the third.

